---
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { MIN_RATING_SOURCES } from '../lib/schemas';
import { SOURCE_PLATFORMS } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="How We Rate Crypto Casinos — Casilla"
  description="The exact formula behind every score on Casilla: which platforms we read, how different scales are reconciled, what we deliberately exclude, and where the method is weak."
>
  <h1 class="text-[32px] sm:text-[38px]">How we rate</h1>

  <Prose>
    <p class="mt-5">
      Casilla does not invent scores. Every number on this site is arithmetic performed on
      figures other people published, and this page describes the arithmetic completely
      enough that you could reproduce it yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>
      For each casino we record the rating currently published by each of these platforms:
      {SOURCE_PLATFORMS.join(', ')}. Each figure is stored with a link to the exact page it
      came from and the date we read it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Platforms use different scales. Trustpilot and LCB publish out of five; the others
      out of ten. Every figure is rebased onto ten by doubling the five-point ones. The
      score we display is the plain, unweighted mean of those rebased numbers, rounded to
      one decimal place.
    </p>
    <p>
      No platform counts for more than any other. We considered weighting — a case can be
      made that a site resolving player complaints deserves more say than one aggregating
      star ratings — and rejected it, because any weighting we chose would be our opinion
      dressed up as a calculation. A plain mean is the one operation nobody has to take on
      trust.
    </p>
    <p>
      A casino found on fewer than {MIN_RATING_SOURCES} platforms is shown as unrated —
      averaging a single figure produces something that looks like a rating and is not
      one. {MIN_RATING_SOURCES} is a lower floor than we started with. Newer casinos are
      often not yet reviewed by every platform we track, and holding them to a stricter
      minimum meant most of them sat unrated indefinitely with no path to a score. Two
      independent figures is still a real average of two different platforms' opinions,
      not one platform's opinion dressed up as a consensus.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not in the score</h2>
    <p>
      Commission is not in the score. Casilla earns money when readers sign up through our
      links, and the ranking order is a direct function of the aggregated figures — there is
      no manual placement, no paid position, and no tie-break we control.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our own editorial opinion is also not in the score. The written verdict on each casino
      page is ours; the number is not. When we think a casino is worse than its aggregate
      suggests, we say so in the text and leave the arithmetic alone.
    </p>

    <h2>Where this method is weak</h2>
    <p>
      Three limitations are worth stating plainly rather than hiding.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It inherits other people's blind spots.</strong> If every platform we read
      rates a casino generously, so do we. The aggregate reduces the effect of one
      platform's bias; it cannot detect a bias they share.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Metrics are not identical.</strong> Casino Guru publishes a Safety Index, which
      weighs complaint history and terms rather than player enjoyment. Trustpilot aggregates
      customer sentiment. Averaging them treats different questions as one, which is a real
      compromise. We label every metric by its actual name in the source table so you can see
      what went into the mean and discount it if you disagree.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It is a snapshot.</strong> Every figure carries the date it was checked. We
      re-verify the leading casinos monthly and update the date only when we have actually
      looked again — a refreshed timestamp on unchanged data would be the easiest lie on this
      site to tell and the least excusable.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If a figure here does not match what the source currently shows, tell us and we will
      check it. Getting a number wrong is the only failure that matters on a site whose whole
      claim is that the numbers are checkable.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Base>
